{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee. I understand you’ve been having some vision changes and a headache today. Can you tell me what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, around 10 this morning, I suddenly lost vision in the left side of my left eye—like from 10 o’clock down. Then everything looked wavy. Shortly after, I got a throbbing headache on the right side and top of my head."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Did you feel nauseous or sensitive to light or sound?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, very sensitive to light and noise, and I felt nauseous. I also had a brief sharp pain in my left eye. I haven’t had a migraine like this since menopause—before that, they were on the right side but never with vision changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You mentioned in the ER that you used to respond to a triptan injection. Is that right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:27:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, a subcutaneous sumatriptan used to help. But I haven’t needed it in years."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:29:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We’ve reviewed your labs. Your sodium is a bit low and potassium is high, which can happen with your Addison’s disease. We’re giving you IV fluids and a higher dose of prednisone to help with that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember last time I was in for something similar—my potassium was high then too. Is this another Addison’s flare?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It looks that way. The low sodium and high potassium support that. We’ve started you on 10 mg of prednisone twice daily—double your usual dose—for stress coverage. We also gave kayexalate to help lower your potassium."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need to stay on that higher dose long?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2166-01-19 23:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just for two more days. Then you’ll go back to your regular 5 mg. Your levels are already improving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2166-01-20 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your head CT came back normal—no bleeding, stroke, or mass. The neurologist and eye doctor both agree your symptoms are consistent with a migraine with aura."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2166-01-20 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So the vision changes were part of the migraine? I was really worried it was a stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2166-01-20 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, the visual symptoms—like the blind spot and wavy lines—are classic for migraine aura. They can happen even if you haven’t had migraines in years. Your exam was normal, and the CT supports this isn’t a stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2166-01-20 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. My headache is much better now. What about the potassium?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2166-01-20 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your potassium was 5.6 when you arrived, but it’s now down to 5.0, which is safe. It improved with fluids and kayexalate. We’ll check it again as an outpatient."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. We’re ready to send you home. We’ll give you sumatriptan injections for future migraines and ketorolac for pain. Keep taking the 10 mg prednisone twice daily for two more days, then return to your usual dose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if my potassium goes up again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re sending you home with a dose of kayexalate to use only if your potassium is high and you feel unwell, but your levels should stabilize as your Addison’s is better controlled."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Please follow up with your primary doctor to review the final CT report and your labs. Also schedule an outpatient MRI to further rule out other causes, even though we’re confident this was a migraine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my clonazepam? I’ve been using it for anxiety and sleep."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue your usual dose of clonazepam as needed. We’ve also adjusted your trazodone for sleep. Everything else, like your thyroid and fludrocortisone, stays the same."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel much better knowing it wasn’t something more serious."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Rest today, avoid triggers like bright lights, and reach out if the headache returns or you feel off."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need to come back if I use the sumatriptan?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, just use it as directed—one injection, and repeat in an hour if needed. But if symptoms are different or worsen, go to the ER."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2166-01-20 15:52:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thanks for explaining everything."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2166-01-19 23:19:00",
    "admission_end": "2166-01-20 16:01:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of Addison’s disease and prior migraines presented with sudden left-sided visual loss followed by wavy vision and a right-sided throbbing headache, accompanied by nausea and photophobia. She was evaluated in the emergency department, where she refused an initial CT but later underwent imaging that showed no hemorrhage, infarct, or mass. Neurology and ophthalmology assessments supported a diagnosis of migraine with aura. Her labs revealed hyponatremia and hyperkalemia, consistent with an Addisonian exacerbation. She was treated with stress-dose prednisone, IV fluids, and kayexalate for hyperkalemia. Her headache and visual symptoms resolved, and she was discharged home with a short course of high-dose steroids, sumatriptan for future migraines, and instructions for outpatient follow-up including an MRI and lab monitoring.",
    "problems": [
      "Migraine with aura, without mention of intractable migraine without mention of status migrainosus",
      "Glucocorticoid deficiency",
      "Hyperpotassemia",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Dysthymic disorder"
    ]
  }
}
